## Choose an interface

The CLI is useful when a person or coding agent works in a terminal. An SDK is useful inside an application. MCP exposes defined capabilities to an agent through its client’s tool interface.

<CodeTabs examples={{TypeScript:'npm install @miosa/sdk',Python:'pip install miosa',Shell:'brew install Miosa-osa/tap/miosa'}} />

These are reference installation examples. Check the provider’s current installation documentation before connecting a production account.

## Configure credentials

Keep a production API key on the server or in the CLI environment. A browser should receive only the limited credentials that its task actually requires.

```bash
export MIOSA_API_KEY="your-key"
```

<Callout title="This site needs no cloud key">Run this reconstruction with <code>npm run dev</code>. Its local learning flows, search and sandbox API work without a MIOSA account.</Callout>

## Verify the selected account

Before creating resources, inspect the account, organization and workspace selected by your client. Make this step explicit in automation so a default profile does not silently choose the wrong environment.

## Use structured output

Prefer structured output when a script needs to read a result. Capture the returned resource or operation identifier and pass that value to the next step. Avoid parsing a human-readable status sentence when a JSON field is available.

## Next steps

